 ## Overview
-This method addresses the instability and incomplete volumetric coverage inherent in optimizing 3D Gaussian representations for volume rendering by structuring training into a curriculum of distinct supervision phases [S1]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Hybrid Coarse-to-Fine Strategy](7173-hybrid-coarse-to-fine-strategy.md) by explicitly decoupling global spatial guidance (via sparse voxels) from local structural continuity (via slices), ensuring that the Gaussian primitives learn both the coarse volumetric backbone and fine anatomical details without the destabilization caused by random sampling or the lack of global context seen in slice-only approaches [S1].
+This method addresses the instability and incomplete volumetric coverage inherent in optimizing 3D Gaussian representations for shear-warp rendering by enforcing a structured training schedule. It differs from sibling methods like [Hybrid Coarse-to-Fine Strategy](7173-hybrid-coarse-to-fine-strategy.md) by explicitly decoupling global spatial guidance (via sparse voxels) from local structural continuity (via slices), rather than relying on hierarchical scene decomposition. It outperforms [Training Objective and Densification](7448-training-objective-and-densification.md) in volumetric consistency by preventing the "unstable global approximation" caused by random voxel sampling alone [S1].
 
 ## How it works
